// Additional original short passages. Answer choices are rotated by the bank builder.
export const readingExpansion=[]
const add=(skill,difficulty,passage,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation)=>readingExpansion.push({skill,difficulty,passage,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation})
const vocab=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Words in Context',d,p,'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',c,w,e)
vocab('hard','The philosopher does not claim that competing explanations are equally persuasive. Her position is more ______: a theory can be better supported than its rivals while still remaining open to revision.','nuanced',['dogmatic','indifferent','categorical'],'Nuanced means attentive to subtle distinctions. The position distinguishes comparative support from certainty. Dogmatic and categorical suggest the rigidity rejected by openness to revision; indifferent would imply not evaluating the rivals.')
vocab('medium','The fictional botanist’s early drawings were often dismissed as decorative. A later comparison with field notes revealed their ______ value: they recorded small differences in leaf shape that the notes never mentioned.','documentary',['monetary','ceremonial','hypothetical'],'The drawings preserve observations, giving them documentary value. The passage makes no claim about price or ritual use; their recorded details are actual features of the fictional collection, not a hypothetical use.')
vocab('hard','The researcher’s request for additional controls was not an attempt to ______ the result; she considered it promising and wanted to distinguish its proposed cause from several plausible alternatives.','disparage',['substantiate','replicate','contextualize'],'Disparage means speak of dismissively. The researcher values the result and seeks stronger causal evidence, so her request is not dismissive. Substantiate and contextualize describe compatible constructive aims rather than the rejected attitude.')
vocab('medium','In the original story, Leon had rehearsed a forceful objection. At the meeting, however, his remarks were unexpectedly ______: he suggested only a minor change and twice thanked the organizer.','restrained',['vehement','irrelevant','exhaustive'],'Restrained means controlled or moderate, matching the modest change and polite thanks. Vehement contradicts the behavior; relevance and exhaustiveness are not the contrast established by the planned forceful objection.')
vocab('hard','The two datasets are not ______. One counts households, the other counts individuals, and neither supplies the information needed to translate between those units.','commensurable',['independent','comprehensive','anomalous'],'Commensurable means measurable or comparable on a common basis. Different units without a conversion prevent that comparison. Independence, completeness, and unusualness are not established by the unit mismatch.')
vocab('medium','The fictional village council made a ______ concession: it moved the concert’s starting time by fifteen minutes but rejected the requested changes to its location and program.','limited',['sweeping','binding','reluctant'],'Limited matches the narrow adjustment relative to the broader request. Sweeping contradicts the small change. The passage does not establish whether the decision is legally binding or emotionally reluctant.')
vocab('hard','The essay’s opening anecdote is not merely ______. The author returns to its details in every section, using them to frame the argument’s central distinctions.','incidental',['integral','illustrative','evocative'],'Incidental means secondary or nonessential, which is what the sentence rejects. The repeated framing makes the anecdote integral. An anecdote can be illustrative or evocative while still playing this central role, so those words do not express the relevant denial.')
vocab('medium','A fictional engineer replaced the prototype’s separate fasteners with a single molded joint. This change ______ assembly: workers no longer had to align and tighten six small pieces.','streamlined',['impeded','outsourced','standardized'],'Streamlined means made simpler or more efficient, precisely the effect of eliminating six assembly steps. Impeded is the opposite; outsourcing changes who performs the work; standardization would require evidence of common specifications.')
vocab('hard','The historian regards the letter’s silences as ______ rather than conclusive: a missing reference may guide further research, but it cannot by itself prove that an event never occurred.','suggestive',['definitive','irrecoverable','fabricated'],'Suggestive means pointing toward an interpretation without establishing it. Definitive conflicts with the stated limit; irrecoverable concerns retrieval, and fabricated concerns false creation, neither of which describes what absence supports.')
vocab('medium','Although the fictional designer’s first plan was inexpensive, it was not ______: repairs required parts that local suppliers could not obtain, making routine maintenance impractical.','viable',['novel','economical','elaborate'],'Viable means capable of working successfully in practice. Inaccessible replacement parts undermine practical feasibility even when initial cost is low. Novelty and complexity are not addressed; economical overlaps the explicitly granted inexpensive price.')
vocab('hard','The scholar rejects the assumption that a text’s meaning is ______ its author’s private intentions; she argues that conventions shared with readers also shape what its words can communicate.','reducible to',['influenced by','independent of','in tension with'],'Reducible to means fully explainable by one factor. Shared conventions challenge the claim that private intentions alone determine meaning. The scholar need not deny that intentions influence meaning, and rejecting independence would reverse her argument.')
vocab('medium','Instead of presenting every recorded detail, the fictional biographer chose anecdotes that ______ the subject’s changing priorities, allowing readers to see how early ambitions gave way to later commitments.','illuminated',['obscured','anticipated','exaggerated'],'Illuminated means clarified or made understandable, matching the anecdotes’ explanatory role. Obscured is the opposite; anticipated concerns prediction; exaggerated would require evidence of distortion.')
vocab('hard','The two researchers’ conclusions are ______ only if one overlooks their different time scales: a short-term decline can coexist with a long-term increase.','contradictory',['complementary','tentative','quantitative'],'Contradictory means inconsistent. The sentence says they seem inconsistent only when the time-scale difference is ignored. Complementary would describe compatibility, not the mistaken impression being conditioned.')
vocab('medium','The fictional city’s old plan treated rainwater as a nuisance to remove quickly. Its new plan takes a more ______ approach, treating stored rainwater as a resource for gardens during dry periods.','resourceful',['dismissive','fatalistic','ceremonial'],'Resourceful means making effective use of available means. Reusing rainwater for gardens exemplifies that quality. The alternatives imply disregard, resignation, or ritual, none supported by the plan.')
vocab('hard','The critic’s praise is ______ by a specific reservation: the exhibition’s labels explain the tools thoroughly but say little about the workers who used them.','tempered',['amplified','precluded','authenticated'],'Tempered means moderated. A reservation limits otherwise positive praise. Amplified would strengthen it; precluded would make praise impossible; authenticated concerns verifying truth or origin.')
vocab('medium','The fictional research team’s records were unusually ______: every altered setting was dated, attributed to a team member, and linked to the measurements it affected.','systematic',['impressionistic','inaccessible','conjectural'],'Systematic means organized according to a clear method. Dated, attributed, linked entries demonstrate that organization. The other words concern subjective impressions, lack of access, or speculation.')

const central=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Central Ideas and Details',d,p,'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',c,w,e)
central('hard','In an imagined study of restoration, conservators compared two repairs to the same kind of ceramic crack. One repair concealed the break almost completely; the other left a thin visible line. Visitors rated the concealed repair as more attractive but were more likely to recognize the object’s history of damage when the line remained. The conservators argued that deciding between repairs required choosing which communicative purpose the display should serve.','Different restoration approaches can serve different exhibition goals, so visual attractiveness alone need not settle the choice.',['Visible repairs are always less historically accurate than concealed repairs.','Most visitors can identify every restoration technique by appearance.','The study showed that all damaged ceramics should be removed from displays.'],'The passage contrasts attractiveness with communicating damage history and makes purpose central to the decision. It does not rank historical accuracy or recommend removing objects.')
central('medium','A fictional town’s neighborhood newsletter stopped grouping announcements by the organizations that submitted them. Instead, it grouped them by the actions readers might take: attend, volunteer, borrow, or apply. Residents reported finding opportunities more easily even though the total number of announcements remained the same.','Reorganizing existing information around readers’ possible actions made the newsletter easier to use.',['The newsletter became useful only after adding more announcements.','Residents preferred notices from larger organizations.','The town replaced printed announcements with private messages.'],'The improvement follows a change in organization rather than content quantity. The text does not compare organization size or describe a change of medium.')
central('hard','In a hypothetical study, a researcher recorded conversations during group problem-solving and afterward asked participants to identify who had contributed each idea. Participants often credited the person who restated an idea clearly rather than the person who first proposed it. The researcher cautioned that retrospective reports of contribution may partly reflect how ideas were presented, not simply where they originated.','Judgments about who contributed an idea may be shaped by its later presentation as well as its original proposal.',['Participants accurately recalled the first speaker of every idea.','Restating an idea is the only valuable contribution to group work.','The researcher found that clear speakers always invented the ideas they summarized.'],'The observed misattribution supports the role of presentation in judgments. The text neither dismisses original proposals nor equates clarity with invention.')
central('easy','In this original story, Imani expects her grandfather to choose a flawless new bowl from her workshop. He picks an uneven one she made during her first lesson. When she offers to replace it, he points to the thumbprint beside the rim and says that a machine could not have left that mark.','Imani’s grandfather values a handmade trace that she initially regards as a flaw.',['Imani’s grandfather believes all new bowls are produced by machines.','Imani refuses to let her grandfather take a bowl.','Imani cannot remember making the uneven bowl.'],'His preference for the early bowl and its thumbprint contrasts with her expectation of perfection. He does not claim all bowls are machine-made, and the other events do not occur.')
central('medium','A fictional field station once required researchers to erase incorrect entries and replace them with corrected values. Its revised protocol asks them to preserve the original entries, mark corrections clearly, and explain each change. The resulting records look less tidy but allow later reviewers to reconstruct how the data were checked.','Preserving a record of corrections can improve the transparency of data review despite making records less visually tidy.',['The station decided that incorrect values should be analyzed as if they were correct.','The new protocol eliminated the need to check measurements.','Reviewers prefer all records to contain no corrections.'],'The revised method documents correction history while distinguishing wrong and corrected values. It improves traceability, not acceptance of error or freedom from checking.')
central('hard','In an imagined oral-history project, interviewers initially transcribed only spoken words. They later added notes about pauses, gestures, and the placement of objects discussed. These additions sometimes changed how a listener’s apparently simple agreement was interpreted: a nod toward a damaged doorway, for example, could introduce a qualification absent from the words alone.','Contextual details can alter the interpretation of oral testimony that a words-only transcript appears to make straightforward.',['Gestures provide an infallible record of a speaker’s true intentions.','Spoken testimony has no value unless recorded on video.','Every apparent agreement in the interviews was actually a disagreement.'],'The examples show that nonverbal context sometimes qualifies verbal meaning. The passage makes no universal claim about gestures, recording requirements, or all agreements.')
central('medium','A fictional orchestra changed its audition process so that evaluators first heard candidates without seeing them. In a second round, evaluators discussed rehearsal needs with the finalists. The director explained that separating these stages allowed musical performance to be considered on its own before practical questions about collaboration were addressed.','The orchestra separated audition stages to evaluate different relevant qualities without letting them all influence the first judgment.',['The orchestra concluded that collaboration was irrelevant to choosing musicians.','Evaluators stopped listening to candidates’ performances.','Every candidate advanced automatically to the second round.'],'Performance is isolated initially, but collaboration remains relevant later. The distractors contradict the two-stage evaluation or invent a universal advancement rule.')
central('hard','An imagined study of route learning found that participants who followed spoken instructions reached a destination quickly on the first attempt. Participants who planned their own route took longer initially but later navigated an unfamiliar detour more successfully. The researchers argued that immediate efficiency and flexible spatial knowledge should be measured separately.','A method that supports quick initial navigation may not support the same degree of later route flexibility.',['Planning a route guarantees the fastest trip on every occasion.','Spoken directions prevented all participants from remembering any landmarks.','The study established that detours are easier than direct routes.'],'The study distinguishes first-attempt speed from later flexible navigation. It supports a task-dependent tradeoff rather than a universal best method or complete loss of memory.')
central('easy','In this original narrative, Sora labels the plants in her courtyard with the names her grandmother used. A neighbor adds another set of names in a different language. Soon the labels hold several names each, and visitors begin exchanging stories about where they first learned them.','Plant labels become a starting point for sharing different languages and personal histories.',['Sora replaces every label with a single official name.','The neighbors disagree about whether the plants should remain in the courtyard.','Visitors use the labels only to learn watering instructions.'],'The accumulating names prompt stories and exchange. The text does not describe replacing them with one naming system, a plant-removal dispute, or watering information.')
central('hard','A fictional scholar studying festival posters noticed that the performers most prominent in the artwork were not always those who received the largest payments. Rather than treating either source as mistaken, she argued that posters and accounts recorded different kinds of importance: visual prominence helped advertise an event, while payments reflected negotiated labor arrangements.','Different historical records may emphasize different forms of importance without being inconsistent or inaccurate.',['Posters provide a precise record of every performer’s payment.','Accounts cannot be used to study artistic labor.','The scholar proved that all festivals used identical advertising strategies.'],'The scholar reconciles the records by identifying their distinct purposes. The other options equate incompatible measures, discard a source, or overgeneralize.')
central('medium','A hypothetical campus study counted both how many students entered a common room and how many returned within a week. A brightly decorated room attracted more first-time visits, while a quieter room with more outlets attracted more repeat visits. The researchers recommended distinguishing initial appeal from sustained usefulness.','First-time visits and repeat visits can reveal different aspects of how well a shared space serves students.',['The room with the most initial visits necessarily had the most repeat visits.','Decorations were the only feature that students noticed.','The researchers recommended removing all electrical outlets.'],'The differing patterns motivate two evaluation measures. The passage does not isolate decoration as the sole cause or propose removing useful facilities.')
central('hard','In an imagined translation workshop, participants produced different versions of the same brief speech. One preserved repeated words; another varied them to sound more natural. When readers compared the versions, the repeated wording made the speaker seem insistent, whereas the varied wording made the speech seem smoother. The workshop treated this difference as evidence that stylistic choices can carry interpretive consequences.','Translation choices about repetition can affect readers’ interpretation of a speaker’s manner.',['The workshop identified a single translation that preserved every possible effect.','Readers failed to notice any differences between versions.','Repeating words is always an error in translation.'],'The readers’ different impressions support the interpretive significance of style. The text neither resolves all tradeoffs nor condemns repetition.')

const structure=(d,p,c,w,e,prompt='Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?')=>add('Text Structure and Purpose',d,p,prompt,c,w,e)
structure('hard','A fictional essay first questions whether a city can be understood from its monuments alone. It next describes delivery routes, informal markets, and repairs to ordinary stairways. It ends by proposing that repeated everyday activities produce a kind of civic history that monumental architecture only partly records.','It challenges a narrow approach, supplies alternative evidence, and offers a broader account.',['It lists monuments and ranks them by the frequency of repairs.','It proposes a theory and then dismisses every example that might support it.','It traces the construction of one monument from planning to completion.'],'The ordinary activities broaden the evidence beyond monuments and support the final account. The text is not a ranking, a rejected theory, or a construction history.')
structure('medium','In a hypothetical experiment, participants remembered lists better when they grouped related words. _The researchers then tested a new list whose categories were deliberately less obvious._ Performance improved only when participants were given time to discover groupings themselves.','It describes a follow-up test designed to probe the conditions under which the initial benefit occurs.',['It states the main conclusion before any evidence is presented.','It supplies an unrelated example of successful memorization.','It establishes that the original study contained fabricated data.'],'The underlined sentence changes how obvious the groupings are to explore the original result. It does not announce a final conclusion, introduce an unrelated topic, or challenge data integrity.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?')
structure('hard','In this original reflection, the narrator calls her mother’s habit of saving empty envelopes excessive. She then discovers that each envelope records an address at which the family once lived. She closes by describing the drawer as a record of departures that no official document had preserved.','It transforms an initial criticism by revealing the personal significance of an ordinary collection.',['It supports an unchanged criticism with increasingly severe examples.','It compares the monetary value of several inherited collections.','It explains why the narrator decides to discard all family records.'],'The envelopes’ meaning changes the narrator’s evaluation. Their value is personal and historical, not monetary; no disposal is described.')
structure('medium','A fictional researcher argues that school maps can omit spaces students consider important. _Several students drew the narrow passage behind the gym, although it appeared on none of the official plans._ The researcher concludes that informal routes may structure students’ experience of a campus.','It provides a concrete observation supporting the argument that official maps can miss meaningful spaces.',['It introduces an exception that disproves the researcher’s argument.','It summarizes every feature included on official school maps.','It shifts the discussion from students to construction contractors.'],'The passage behind the gym is both student-recognized and officially absent, directly illustrating the argument. The sentence does not disprove it or change the participants.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?')
structure('hard','An imagined reviewer distinguishes disagreement about a novel’s events from disagreement about its values. She notes that two critics recount the plot almost identically but interpret the protagonist’s final refusal in opposite ways. She concludes that their dispute concerns the meaning of a shared description, not conflicting accounts of what happened.','It introduces a distinction, illustrates it with a comparison, and applies the distinction to explain a dispute.',['It reconstructs two incompatible plot summaries and selects the more accurate one.','It explains the historical origin of the novel’s publication title.','It abandons a conceptual distinction because the critics agree on everything.'],'The critics share a plot account but differ in evaluation, so the opening distinction explains their dispute. Agreement on events does not imply agreement on values.')
structure('easy','In this original story, a child hears a whistle from the locked shed each evening. She waits beside the door to investigate. When the wind rises, air passes through a cracked pipe and produces the sound she had imagined was a hidden visitor.','It establishes a mystery, describes an investigation, and gives a physical explanation.',['It reports a conversation between two hidden visitors.','It begins with a scientific explanation and then rejects it.','It describes a problem that remains entirely unresolved.'],'The wind and pipe explain the whistle after the child investigates. No hidden visitor appears and the mystery is resolved.')
structure('hard','A hypothetical report says that a single annual average can conceal seasonal shortages. _A reservoir with a high annual average may still fall below demand for several consecutive summer weeks._ The report therefore recommends publishing the timing of low levels as well as yearly summaries.','It illustrates how the measure criticized in the first sentence can fail to reveal a practical problem.',['It shows that the report’s recommendation has already solved all shortages.','It establishes that annual averages cannot be computed for reservoirs.','It offers an exception in which seasonal timing never matters.'],'The reservoir example concretely shows what an annual average can hide. It neither claims impossibility of calculation nor reports success of a policy.','What is the main purpose of the underlined sentence?')
structure('medium','A fictional catalog initially groups woven baskets by the fibers they contain. It then describes baskets made from the same fiber but used for carrying grain, filtering liquid, and storing clothing. It closes by explaining why a second index organized by use helps readers ask different questions of the collection.','It presents one classification, demonstrates differences that it does not emphasize, and motivates an additional classification.',['It proves that fiber identification is impossible.','It lists uses of baskets to argue that all baskets are interchangeable.','It replaces every material description with a price estimate.'],'The different uses show why material alone is not the only useful organizing principle. The added index complements rather than invalidates fiber classification.')
structure('hard','In a hypothetical history essay, an author explains that official opening dates do not always mark the start of a public service. She describes a bridge used by pedestrians for months before its ceremonial opening, then examines why officials chose a later date for the ceremony.','It complicates a familiar assumption through a specific case and then investigates the case’s official presentation.',['It lists the opening dates of unrelated bridges without interpretation.','It treats the ceremonial date as proof that no earlier use occurred.','It argues that every public ceremony has no historical value.'],'The example separates practical use from ceremony and prompts investigation of official framing. The author neither ignores earlier use nor dismisses ceremonies as evidence.')
structure('medium','A fictional craft historian describes a repaired chair whose replacement leg differs from the others. _The mismatch makes the repair visible, but it also shows how the chair was kept in use with available materials._ The historian treats the chair as evidence of maintenance rather than merely of original manufacture.','It reframes an apparent defect as evidence relevant to the historian’s interpretation.',['It proves that the chair was never repaired.','It explains why the chair must be an exact copy of an earlier object.','It dismisses maintenance as irrelevant to historical study.'],'The sentence moves from visual mismatch to its evidentiary value for repair and reuse. The alternatives contradict that reframing.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?')
structure('hard','An imagined linguistics article begins with a puzzle: listeners can understand an unfamiliar phrase even when none of its words has changed meaning. It then analyzes how word order and surrounding conversation restrict possible interpretations. It ends by arguing that meaning depends on relations among familiar elements as well as on the elements themselves.','It poses a puzzle, examines mechanisms that explain it, and draws a general conclusion.',['It introduces an unfamiliar language and supplies a complete dictionary.','It offers several unrelated puzzles without proposing an explanation.','It rejects the role of context after showing that word order has no effect.'],'Word order and conversational context explain the initial puzzle and support the relational conclusion. The text does not provide a dictionary or reject context.')
structure('easy','A fictional community group wanted a quieter meeting room. It measured street noise near several windows, selected the least noisy wall, and moved the chairs there. Participants then reported hearing one another more easily.','It identifies a need, describes an evidence-based adjustment, and reports an outcome.',['It describes several failed changes without reaching a result.','It argues that measurements cannot inform room layout.','It compares two groups that never use the same room.'],'The need leads to measurement and relocation, followed by improved reported hearing. The other descriptions misrepresent the procedure or result.')

const inference=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Inferences',d,p,'Which choice most logically follows from the text?',c,w,e)
inference('hard','In a hypothetical experiment, participants who saw a sequence of isolated images remembered more individual objects than participants who saw the same objects embedded in scenes. The scene group, however, remembered more accurately which objects had appeared together. The researchers caution against describing either presentation as simply better for memory.','The relative benefit of a presentation depends on which aspect of memory is assessed.',['Embedding objects in scenes necessarily weakens every form of memory.','The two groups remembered identical information with different confidence.','Isolated images preserve relationships among objects better than scenes do.'],'Object recall and relational recall favor different conditions, so the evaluation depends on the target measure. The alternatives erase or reverse the reported difference.')
inference('medium','A fictional restoration team finds two layers of paint on a sign. The lower layer contains a shop name absent from surviving business directories. The team knows that the directories omitted some short-lived businesses.','The shop’s absence from the directories does not establish that the lower painted name was never used.',['The lower name must have been invented by the restoration team.','Every name absent from the directories belonged to a short-lived business.','The directories provide a complete record of all businesses in the district.'],'Known omissions make absence inconclusive. They do not prove the name’s history or justify a universal classification of missing names.')
inference('hard','In an imagined study, researchers compare soil samples collected from the same plots in different decades. The early samples were stored in paper envelopes; later samples were sealed in plastic. A substance of interest can change concentration during storage.','Storage differences need to be considered before attributing all measured differences to changes in the plots.',['The plots’ soil composition could not have changed between decades.','Plastic storage necessarily increases the substance’s concentration.','The original collectors deliberately selected unrepresentative plots.'],'Storage may affect the measured outcome and varies with collection period, creating an alternative explanation. Its direction and intentional bias are not established.')
inference('medium','In this original narrative, Rosa tells her cousin she has no use for the old train timetable. After he leaves, she unfolds it carefully and traces the route to a town she has not mentioned in years.','The timetable may hold personal significance that Rosa did not reveal to her cousin.',['Rosa has forgotten how to interpret printed timetables.','Rosa intends to leave on the next train that evening.','Rosa’s cousin knows exactly why she studies the route.'],'Her private attention contrasts with her public dismissal, supporting unspoken significance. A specific travel plan or the cousin’s knowledge would require additional evidence.')
inference('hard','A hypothetical committee evaluates grant proposals using independent reviewers. Reviewers agree closely on technical feasibility but disagree on public value. The committee publishes only a single average score for each proposal.','The published averages may conceal which evaluation dimension produced disagreement.',['Every proposal received the same technical-feasibility score.','Averaging eliminates the reviewers’ underlying disagreement.','Public value is objectively impossible to evaluate.'],'One combined number can hide agreement on one dimension and disagreement on another. Close agreement within dimensions does not imply equal scores across all proposals.')
inference('easy','A fictional museum’s audio tour includes a transcript for every recording. Visitors can open the transcripts on their phones without playing the sound.','A visitor can access the tour’s spoken content without listening to its recordings.',['Visitors must bring headphones to read the transcripts.','The transcripts contain information absent from every recording.','The museum no longer permits sound in its galleries.'],'The stated transcript access directly supports a silent way to obtain the spoken content. The other options impose conditions or claim content differences not provided.')
inference('hard','In a hypothetical breeding study, a trait becomes common in a small isolated population. Researchers lack records of whether individuals with the trait left more offspring, and random changes in trait frequency are possible in such populations.','The observed increase alone is insufficient to show that the trait improved reproductive success.',['The trait necessarily reduced reproductive success.','Random frequency changes are impossible once a trait becomes common.','Every individual acquired the trait during its own lifetime.'],'Selection is not the only possible explanation for a frequency shift. Without reproductive evidence, the increase does not establish an advantage or its opposite.')
inference('medium','A fictional theater reduces ticket prices for weekday shows. Attendance rises, but the theater also begins offering free transport on those days. It changes neither policy separately during the study.','The observed increase cannot be attributed uniquely to lower ticket prices from these data alone.',['Free transport had no possible effect on attendance.','Lower prices necessarily reduced total attendance.','The study proves that both policies contributed equally.'],'Both interventions changed together, so their effects are not separated. The data do not establish zero effects, negative effects, or equal contributions.')
inference('hard','A hypothetical catalog dates anonymous sketches by comparing the paper with dated notebooks. The same paper stock was sold for many years, and artists sometimes retained unused sheets for decades.','A match in paper stock can inform dating without fixing the exact year a sketch was made.',['Paper stock has no possible relevance to dating a sketch.','Every sketch on matching paper was drawn in the notebook’s year.','Artists always used paper immediately after buying it.'],'The long sale and retention periods limit temporal precision but do not make paper evidence worthless. The other options convert a possible clue into certainty or reject it completely.')
inference('medium','A fictional city’s heat survey measures air temperature at noon along two streets. Residents report that one street feels much hotter in late afternoon, when its west-facing walls receive direct sun. The survey collects no afternoon readings.','The noon survey may miss a time-specific difference relevant to residents’ experience.',['Residents’ experiences must be wrong because the survey used thermometers.','The streets necessarily have identical temperatures throughout the day.','West-facing walls always make every street hotter at noon.'],'The measured time does not cover the reported condition. The text does not invalidate residents, establish all-day equality, or state a universal orientation effect.')
inference('hard','In an imagined study of essays, evaluators rated unsigned texts lower when they believed the writers were beginners. The texts were actually identical across the beginner and expert conditions.','Information about presumed authorship affected evaluations independently of the texts’ wording.',['The beginner writers used less precise vocabulary than the experts.','The evaluators detected genuine differences in essay content.','The study establishes that author experience never influences actual writing.'],'Identical texts isolate presumed authorship as a factor in ratings. The findings concern evaluation, not actual differences between writers or the irrelevance of experience.')
inference('medium','In this original story, Dev repeatedly checks the new lock he installed for his neighbor. When she asks whether anything is wrong, he says the mechanism works perfectly, then asks her to try turning the key herself.','Dev may want reassurance that the lock works comfortably for its intended user, beyond his own mechanical checks.',['Dev knows the lock cannot be opened with its key.','The neighbor has already refused to use the lock.','Dev installed the lock without testing it at all.'],'His invitation to the user after his own checks supports concern with her practical experience. It does not prove a defect, prior refusal, or lack of testing.')
inference('hard','Two hypothetical studies report different average growth rates for the same plant species. One reports growth per day during a favorable season; the other averages over a full year that includes dormancy.','The different averaging periods could explain the different reported rates without implying contradictory measurements.',['The annual study must contain incorrect arithmetic.','Dormancy makes every daily measurement in the seasonal study invalid.','The studies necessarily sampled two different species.'],'An annual average includes low-growth periods omitted from the seasonal average. The difference can therefore arise from scope rather than error or species identity.')
inference('easy','A fictional lending program allows members to reserve a tool online and collect it from any of three neighborhood depots. Each reservation lists the selected collection location.','Members do not all have to collect tools from one central depot.',['Reserved tools are delivered automatically to members’ homes.','Every tool is always available at every depot.','Reservations omit information about collection locations.'],'The explicit choice of three depots supports decentralized collection. Availability and home delivery are not stated, and the final option contradicts the reservation details.')
inference('hard','An imagined historian finds that a regulation was copied into several successive law books. Court records from the same period, however, rarely mention its enforcement. Copying may reflect either continuing relevance or the routine reproduction of earlier texts.','Repeated appearance in law books alone does not establish how actively the regulation was enforced.',['The regulation was never enforced under any circumstances.','Court records necessarily preserve every enforcement action.','Each reprinting introduced an entirely new regulation.'],'Routine copying provides an alternative explanation for textual persistence. Sparse court mentions limit certainty but do not prove zero enforcement or complete record coverage.')
inference('medium','A hypothetical memory aid improves performance when participants are tested immediately after studying. The researchers have not yet tested delayed recall. A school wants to know whether the aid improves retention over a month.','The immediate-test result does not by itself answer the school’s question about month-long retention.',['The aid is certain to become harmful after a month.','Immediate recall and delayed recall always have identical outcomes.','The aid cannot improve any form of memory.'],'The school’s outcome differs in timing from the measured outcome. Further delayed testing is needed; neither harm nor equivalence nor total ineffectiveness follows.')

const cross=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Cross-Text Connections',d,p,'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?',c,w,e)
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined critic argues that a faithful translation should preserve repeated words because repetition reveals how a speaker develops an argument.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother critic agrees that repetition can matter but notes that repeating one word in the source language may require several different words in the target language. She proposes preserving the rhetorical pattern even when exact word repetition changes.','By distinguishing preservation of a rhetorical effect from mechanical repetition of identical words.',['By denying that repetition can contribute to an argument.','By insisting that every repeated source word must always have one target equivalent.','By treating all differences between languages as irrelevant.'],'Text 2 accepts the effect’s importance but questions a one-word-to-one-word method. It refines what faithful preservation requires rather than dismissing repetition.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional planner recommends comparing libraries by annual borrowing totals, reasoning that more borrowing indicates greater public value.\n\n**Text 2**\nA second planner notes that libraries also provide meeting space, internet access, and help completing forms. A branch could serve many residents who borrow no books.','By arguing that the proposed measure omits several ways libraries serve the public.',['By asserting that borrowing totals cannot be counted.','By agreeing that nonborrowing users receive no public benefit.','By claiming that branches with fewer loans are always more valuable.'],'Text 2 broadens the relevant services without denying borrowing’s value or providing a universal inverse ranking.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined scientist attributes a newly observed pattern to a proposed mechanism because the mechanism predicts the pattern.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother scientist notes that two alternative mechanisms make the same prediction. She recommends designing a test whose results would differ across the mechanisms.','By questioning whether the observation distinguishes the proposed mechanism from plausible alternatives.',['By claiming that a correct prediction never supplies any evidence.','By accepting the mechanism solely because the pattern was observed.','By denying that different mechanisms can ever share predictions.'],'Text 2 identifies underdetermination and calls for discriminating evidence. It does not reject all predictive evidence, accept the original inference unchanged, or deny shared predictions.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional curator says an object’s wear should be removed because damage distracts from the maker’s original design.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother curator says wear can document later users’ activities. A polished handle and repaired seam may reveal a history that the original design alone does not record.','By arguing that traces of use can have historical value independent of original design.',['By denying that objects have original designs.','By claiming that every damaged object has the same use history.','By agreeing that all wear should be removed before study.'],'The second author adds users’ history as another value. The response does not eliminate original design or generalize all wear into the same story.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined researcher recommends evaluating a navigation system by the average time needed to complete routes.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother researcher observes that two systems can have the same average while one occasionally produces very long delays. She recommends reporting the distribution of times as well.','By noting that the average may hide important differences in reliability.',['By arguing that route completion time is never relevant.','By asserting that equal averages imply identical individual times.','By replacing time measurements with counts of map colors.'],'Text 2 retains time as relevant but adds variation and extreme delays. It challenges sufficiency of the average, not the relevance of time itself.')
cross('easy','**Text 1**\nA fictional teacher says students should read a poem silently before discussing it so they can form individual interpretations.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother teacher recommends the same silent reading, followed by reading aloud to notice rhythms that may be less obvious on the page.','By keeping the initial activity and adding another activity with a different purpose.',['By rejecting silent reading as useless.','By proposing that students discuss the poem without reading it.','By claiming that rhythm is the only meaningful feature of poetry.'],'Text 2 explicitly retains silent reading and adds oral reading for rhythm. The other responses misstate the proposal or its scope.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined historian treats a town’s printed ordinances as a direct description of how residents behaved.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother historian argues that repeated prohibitions may reveal persistent behavior authorities wanted to stop, rather than behavior they successfully eliminated.','By distinguishing official intentions from actual practices and offering an alternative reading of prohibitions.',['By insisting that written rules contain no historical information.','By claiming that every prohibition was followed immediately and completely.','By arguing that all residents wrote the ordinances together.'],'Text 2 treats ordinances as evidence requiring interpretation, particularly of unwanted persistence. It neither discards them nor equates prescription with compliance.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional manufacturer says the lightest backpack is best because carrying less weight reduces effort.\n\n**Text 2**\nA reviewer notes that strap fit and load distribution also affect comfort. A slightly heavier pack may be easier for a particular user to carry.','By arguing that weight is relevant but insufficient to determine which pack works best for an individual.',['By denying that weight can affect effort at all.','By concluding that the heaviest backpack is always best.','By agreeing that fit has no bearing on comfort.'],'The reviewer introduces additional factors and user variation. She does not deny weight’s relevance or reverse the manufacturer’s ranking universally.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined editor says footnotes should explain every unfamiliar reference so readers never need to consult another source.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother editor argues that exhaustive notes may overwhelm the main text. She favors notes that clarify references essential to following the argument and links for optional exploration.','By proposing a selective approach that distinguishes essential explanation from optional detail.',['By recommending that every unfamiliar reference remain unexplained.','By asserting that readers cannot follow any text with footnotes.','By agreeing that exhaustive explanation has no costs.'],'The response balances explanatory needs against overload. It limits rather than abolishes notes and recognizes a cost denied by the third distractor.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional researcher interprets increased attendance after a museum’s redesign as evidence that the redesigned exhibits caused the increase.\n\n**Text 2**\nA second researcher notes that a new rail station opened beside the museum at the same time, making visits easier.','By identifying another contemporaneous change that could help explain the attendance increase.',['By proving that the exhibit redesign reduced attendance.','By showing that the museum collected no attendance data.','By concluding that transport access can never affect museum visits.'],'The rail station is a competing explanation that weakens exclusive attribution. It does not establish a negative redesign effect or absence of data.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined literary critic says a novel’s unresolved ending reflects the author’s failure to choose a coherent conclusion.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother critic notes that the novel repeatedly asks characters to act without full knowledge. The open ending, she argues, puts readers in the same uncertain position.','By interpreting the ending as an intentional extension of a pattern developed throughout the novel.',['By denying that the ending leaves anything unresolved.','By agreeing that uncertainty appears only because the author forgot earlier events.','By treating the ending as unrelated to the characters’ experiences.'],'The second critic connects reader uncertainty with the novel’s recurring theme, interpreting openness as purposeful rather than incoherent.')
cross('easy','**Text 1**\nA fictional gardener prefers flowers that bloom at once because they create a vivid display.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother gardener prefers staggered blooming, noting that it provides flowers for a longer portion of the season.','By favoring a different planting schedule because it serves a different goal.',['By claiming that simultaneous blooms cannot look vivid.','By agreeing that display duration never matters.','By denying that gardens can contain more than one flower variety.'],'The authors value concentrated visual impact and extended duration respectively. The second need not reject the first benefit to prioritize another.')

const textual=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Command of Evidence (Textual)',d,p,`Which finding, if true, would most directly ${task}?`,c,w,e)
textual('hard','In a hypothetical study, researchers propose that a bird species uses reflected moonlight to orient its nighttime flights. They predict disrupted orientation when the sky is fully overcast.','weaken the researchers’ hypothesis','Birds maintain equally accurate orientation in fully overcast and clear conditions when other cues are held constant.',['The birds fly at night in more than one season.','Moonlight intensity differs across phases of the moon.','The species has larger eyes than a related daytime species.'],'Accurate orientation when the proposed cue is unavailable directly challenges dependence on reflected moonlight. General observations about activity, moon phases, or anatomy do not test that dependence.')
textual('hard','A fictional historian proposes that merchants chose a particular harbor mainly because its storage fees were lower than those of nearby harbors.','weaken the historian’s explanation','Merchants continued favoring the harbor during a long period when its storage fees were substantially higher than nearby alternatives.',['The harbor maintained written records of storage payments.','Some merchants transported more goods than others.','Several other harbors operated in the region.'],'Continued preference despite higher fees undermines low fees as the main explanation. The other facts establish context without contradicting the proposed cost advantage.')
textual('medium','In a hypothetical experiment, a team claims that brighter labels make a display easier to navigate because they are more visible.','weaken the team’s explanation','When label wording and placement are identical, brighter labels do not improve navigation compared with less bright labels.',['Visitors differ in how often they attend museums.','The display includes objects of several sizes.','Some visitors prefer the brighter labels’ appearance.'],'A controlled comparison showing no navigation benefit directly challenges the proposed visibility effect. Appearance preferences are not the claimed navigation outcome.')
textual('hard','In an imagined investigation, researchers attribute a rise in plant growth to a new soil additive. Treated plots were also watered more frequently than untreated plots.','weaken the attribution to the additive','With watering held equal, plots with and without the additive grow at the same rate.',['The additive has a different color from the soil.','Some plots contain more than one plant species.','Workers applied the additive using several kinds of containers.'],'Equal growth under matched watering suggests the original difference may have come from the watering confound. Color and containers do not directly resolve causation.')
textual('hard','A fictional scholar argues that an anonymous letter was written by a particular official because it uses an unusual closing phrase found in that official’s signed letters.','weaken the proposed identification','The same closing phrase appears in a widely circulated writing manual used by many officials of the period.',['The anonymous letter and signed letters were written in the same language.','The official sometimes wrote more than one letter in a day.','The anonymous letter discusses a public matter.'],'A shared manual makes the phrase less distinctive as evidence of authorship. The other facts do not show that other writers would use the same closing.')
textual('medium','In a hypothetical reading study, researchers propose that a new font improves comprehension rather than merely reading speed.','weaken the proposed comprehension benefit','Readers using the font finish sooner but answer no more comprehension questions correctly than readers using the old font.',['The new font has wider letters.','Some readers describe the new font as attractive.','The study uses passages on several subjects.'],'Faster completion without improved understanding distinguishes speed from comprehension and challenges the latter claim. Design and preferences alone do not establish comprehension.')
textual('hard','An imagined ecologist proposes that fewer insects are found beside a road because traffic noise drives them away, rather than because the roadside vegetation differs.','weaken the noise explanation','When identical vegetation is used in quiet and traffic-noise plots, insect counts are similar, while changing vegetation changes the counts.',['The road carries traffic during both day and night.','Some insects are difficult to identify by sight.','The region has several roads with different speed limits.'],'The controlled results favor vegetation over noise. The other observations do not separate those candidate explanations.')
textual('hard','A fictional researcher claims that a decline in recorded workshop injuries shows that workshops became safer after a reporting form changed.','weaken the safety conclusion','The new form excludes several types of minor injury that the old form required workshops to report.',['The new form uses less paper than the old form.','The workshops make several different products.','Managers prefer the new form’s layout.'],'A narrower reporting definition can lower recorded injuries without reducing actual injuries. Paper use and layout preference do not explain the count’s comparability.')
textual('medium','In a hypothetical study, researchers suggest that arranging tasks by dependency helps teams notice which delays will affect later work.','support the researchers’ hypothesis','Teams using dependency diagrams more accurately identify which missed deadlines would postpone other tasks than teams using unordered lists.',['The diagrams contain more lines than the lists.','Teams vary in how many members they contain.','Participants recognize several common task names.'],'The result measures recognition of downstream effects, exactly the hypothesized benefit. Visual complexity and background variation do not establish it.')
textual('hard','A fictional archaeologist proposes that a path served everyday local movement rather than exclusively ceremonial processions.','support the archaeologist’s interpretation','Wear patterns connect the path directly to ordinary houses, wells, and work areas used throughout the year.',['The path has an unusual ceremonial monument at one end.','The path is described in a modern tourist guide.','The stones vary slightly in color.'],'Connections to routine activities and year-round use support an everyday function. A ceremonial endpoint alone does not distinguish exclusive ceremonial use from mixed use.')
textual('medium','In an imagined experiment, a team proposes that a pause before answering helps participants check an initially tempting but incorrect response.','support the proposed checking mechanism','Participants required to pause more often replace an initial incorrect response with a correct one before submitting it.',['Participants in both groups understand the instructions.','The pause group takes longer to finish the test.','Some questions have longer wording than others.'],'Observed correction during the pause directly supports checking. Longer completion time is a consequence of waiting but does not show that responses were re-evaluated.')
textual('hard','A fictional historian argues that a craft technique spread through traveling apprentices rather than through printed manuals alone.','support the historian’s argument','Workshop records trace apprentices moving between towns before the technique appears there, including towns without the relevant manuals.',['Several manuals describe other craft techniques.','The towns have different numbers of workshops.','Printed manuals sometimes contain illustrations.'],'The timing of apprentice movement and adoption where manuals were absent supports the proposed route. General features of manuals do not establish transmission.')
const literary=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Command of Evidence (Textual)',d,p,'Which quotation from the text most directly supports the stated interpretation?',c,w,e)
literary('medium','The following is an original narrative. A reader interprets Nella as valuing continuity despite outward change.\n\nNella returned to the bakery after twenty years. The counter was steel now, and the old blue door had been replaced by glass. She listened while the baker counted coins. “You still pause before saying the total,” she told him, smiling. She bought the smallest loaf and carried it home unwrapped.','“You still pause before saying the total,” she told him, smiling.',['“The counter was steel now”','“the old blue door had been replaced by glass”','“She bought the smallest loaf”'],'Her pleased recognition of a persisting habit directly supports valuing continuity. The changes establish contrast, while the loaf’s size does not identify what she values.')
literary('hard','The following is an original narrative. A reader claims that the narrator’s apparent confidence conceals anxiety.\n\nI told the crew the repair was routine. I laid the tools in a straight line and explained each step without hesitation. When they left to fetch the cable, I counted the tools again, then again, though none had moved. At their return I picked up the wrench and resumed the explanation.','“I counted the tools again, then again, though none had moved.”',['“I told the crew the repair was routine.”','“I laid the tools in a straight line”','“I picked up the wrench”'],'Unnecessary repeated checking in private is the strongest sign of anxiety beneath confident public explanation. The alternatives chiefly show the outward competence the interpretation contrasts with.')
literary('medium','The following is an original narrative. A reader interprets the repaired chair as connecting the narrator to her aunt.\n\nThe chair wobbled despite my careful work. I tightened the new screw and sat down cautiously. The creak it made was the same one that had followed my aunt’s stories every Sunday. I left the screw as it was and brought my book to the window.','“The creak it made was the same one that had followed my aunt’s stories every Sunday.”',['“The chair wobbled despite my careful work.”','“I tightened the new screw”','“I brought my book to the window.”'],'The repeated creak explicitly links the present object with memories of the aunt. The other lines concern repair or current activity without making that connection.')
literary('hard','The following is an original narrative. A reader argues that Farid’s criticism includes concern for the young designer.\n\nFarid marked the drawing with a blunt pencil. “This joint will fail,” he said. The designer folded her arms. He turned the page and began sketching a support. “Build this version first,” he said, “and I’ll bring the timber.”','“Build this version first,” he said, “and I’ll bring the timber.”',['“Farid marked the drawing with a blunt pencil.”','“This joint will fail,” he said.','“The designer folded her arms.”'],'Offering materials for a safer revision demonstrates practical support accompanying criticism. The warning alone could be critical without revealing concern, and the designer’s reaction does not establish Farid’s motive.')

const quantitative=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Command of Evidence (Quantitative)',d,p,task,c,w,e)
quantitative('hard','In a hypothetical study, a researcher claims that training improved accuracy more on difficult tasks than on easy tasks.\n\n| Task | Before training (%) | After training (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Easy | 84 | 91 |\n| Difficult | 42 | 66 |','Which choice most effectively uses the table to support the claim?','Accuracy increased by 24 percentage points on difficult tasks, compared with 7 on easy tasks.',['After training, easy-task accuracy exceeded difficult-task accuracy by 25 points.','Before training, difficult-task accuracy was 42%.','Easy-task accuracy was higher both before and after training.'],'The claim compares improvements: 66−42=24 and 91−84=7. Final levels or rankings do not establish the relative changes.')
quantitative('hard','A hypothetical report claims that longer hours increased total visits without increasing average visits per open hour.\n\n| Period | Open hours per week | Visits per week |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before | 30 | 900 |\n| After | 40 | 1200 |','Which choice most effectively uses the table to support the claim?','Visits increased from 900 to 1,200 while visits per open hour remained 30.',['Hours increased by 10, so visits per hour increased by 10.','Visits rose by 300, so the rate per hour must also have risen.','There were more visits than open hours in each period.'],'Divide totals by hours: 900/30=30 and 1200/40=30. More total visits can coexist with an unchanged hourly rate.')
quantitative('medium','In a hypothetical survey, researchers claim that a tool was more widely adopted in small workshops but more frequently used among adopters in large workshops.\n\n| Workshop size | Using the tool (%) | Weekly uses per adopter |\n|---|---|---|\n| Small | 70 | 3 |\n| Large | 45 | 8 |','Which choice most effectively supports both parts of the claim?','Adoption was 70% in small workshops versus 45% in large ones, but adopters averaged 3 versus 8 weekly uses respectively.',['Small workshops had both higher adoption and more weekly uses per adopter.','Large workshops averaged 8 weekly uses per adopter.','Adoption in small workshops exceeded 50%.'],'The correct option distinguishes prevalence from use intensity and accurately reports both opposing comparisons. The partial statements omit a required part.')
quantitative('hard','A hypothetical researcher claims that a supplement improves growth equally under all light levels.\n\n| Light | Growth without supplement (cm) | Growth with supplement (cm) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Low | 4 | 10 |\n| High | 14 | 15 |','Which choice uses the table to challenge the claim most directly?','The supplement was associated with a 6 cm increase under low light but only a 1 cm increase under high light.',['Growth with the supplement was greater under high light than low light.','Unsupplemented plants grew 14 cm under high light.','Supplemented plants grew at least 10 cm in both conditions.'],'Unequal within-light gains directly contradict equal improvement. Different final levels could arise from baseline differences and do not alone measure the supplement’s gain.')
quantitative('medium','A hypothetical accessibility team claims that a redesign reduced errors without requiring more time.\n\n| Design | Mean errors | Mean completion time (s) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Original | 6 | 80 |\n| Revised | 2 | 77 |','Which choice most effectively supports the claim?','Mean errors fell from 6 to 2, while mean completion time fell from 80 to 77 seconds.',['The revised design had 2 errors and therefore required more time.','The original design took 80 seconds on average.','Both designs had completion times below 100 seconds.'],'Both needed outcomes are reported: fewer errors and no time increase. The first distractor contradicts the recorded decrease in time.')
quantitative('hard','A hypothetical ecologist claims that site A showed greater proportional recovery than site B.\n\n| Site | Initial count | Later count |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 20 | 50 |\n| B | 100 | 160 |','Which choice most effectively supports the claim?','A’s count increased by 150%, compared with B’s 60% increase.',['B gained 60 individuals while A gained 30.','B had a higher count at both measurements.','A’s later count was 50 and B’s was 160.'],'Proportional growth uses the initial count: 30/20=150% and 60/100=60%. Absolute gains favor B but answer a different question.')
quantitative('medium','In a fictional materials test, an engineer says neither tested coating eliminated water absorption, although both reduced it.\n\n| Coating | Water absorbed (g) |\n|---|---|\n| None | 18 |\n| A | 7 |\n| B | 3 |','Which choice most effectively supports the engineer’s statement?','Absorption was below the uncoated 18 g for both A and B, but remained above zero at 7 g and 3 g.',['B absorbed less water than A.','The uncoated sample absorbed 18 g.','Both coatings eliminated at least 18 g of absorption.'],'The correct option establishes both reduction and nonelimination. Ranking the coatings alone does not demonstrate both points, and neither reduction reached 18 g.')
quantitative('hard','A hypothetical historian claims that the later archive period had fewer missing records as a proportion of all expected records.\n\n| Period | Expected records | Missing records |\n|---|---|---|\n| Earlier | 100 | 15 |\n| Later | 400 | 24 |','Which choice most effectively supports the claim?','Missing records fell from 15% of the earlier total to 6% of the later total.',['The later period had 9 more missing records.','The later period had four times as many expected records.','The earlier period had 15 missing records.'],'Use each period’s own expected total: 15/100=15% and 24/400=6%. A higher missing count can still be a lower missing proportion.')
quantitative('medium','A hypothetical researcher claims that method X is faster but less accurate than method Y.\n\n| Method | Mean time (min) | Accuracy (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| X | 12 | 81 |\n| Y | 17 | 94 |','Which choice most effectively supports the claim?','X required 5 fewer minutes but had accuracy 13 percentage points lower than Y.',['X took 12 minutes and was more accurate than Y.','Y was 13 minutes slower than X.','Both methods had accuracy above 80%.'],'The correct statement gives both relevant comparisons: 17−12=5 minutes and 94−81=13 percentage points. The distractors reverse, miscalculate, or omit the tradeoff.')
quantitative('hard','In a hypothetical study, a researcher says that the reminder effect depended on whether participants had already registered.\n\n| Status | Completion without reminder (%) | Completion with reminder (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Registered | 62 | 83 |\n| Unregistered | 19 | 21 |','Which choice most directly supports the researcher’s statement?','Reminders increased completion by 21 points among registered participants but by only 2 among unregistered participants.',['Registered participants had higher completion rates under both conditions.','Completion with reminders was 83% among registered participants.','Unregistered participants’ completion was below 25% in both conditions.'],'Different within-status effects support dependence on registration status. Between-status levels alone could differ even if reminder effects were identical.')
quantitative('medium','A hypothetical exhibition report claims that adding context increased discussion time more than reading time.\n\n| Label | Mean reading time (s) | Mean discussion time (s) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Brief | 20 | 30 |\n| Context-rich | 28 | 65 |','Which choice most effectively supports the claim?','Discussion time increased by 35 seconds, while reading time increased by 8 seconds.',['Both reading and discussion took more than 20 seconds with context-rich labels.','Context-rich labels took 28 seconds to read.','Brief labels had a discussion time of 30 seconds.'],'The claim compares changes, which are 65−30=35 and 28−20=8. Isolated times do not establish the larger effect.')
quantitative('hard','A fictional field team claims a new sampling method found more rare species without increasing the total number of species detected.\n\n| Method | Total species detected | Rare species detected |\n|---|---|---|\n| Old | 40 | 3 |\n| New | 40 | 9 |','Which choice most effectively supports the claim?','The new method detected 9 rare species versus 3 for the old method, while both detected 40 species overall.',['The new method detected 6 more species overall.','The old method detected fewer than 40 species.','Both methods detected at least one rare species.'],'The total remains fixed at 40 while rare detections triple. This supports a change in the composition of detections, not an increase in total count.')
